//
//  1. StartBankUpdate returns a link token for the link.
//  2. The app opens Plaid Link with it. The user signs in or consents again,
//     and may share more accounts at the bank or stop sharing some.
//  3. CompleteBankUpdate brings Flipcash's accounts for the link in line
//     with what the user now shares, and returns the link.
//
// A single account is removed by unticking it in an update: the user then no
// longer shares it with Flipcash at Plaid, and Flipcash removes it.
// RemoveBankLink removes a whole bank. Besides RemoveBankLink, Flipcash
// removes a link only when it is gone at Plaid (see below): a link at which
// the user shares no account any more is kept, with no accounts (see
// CompleteBankUpdate).
//
// A link is gone at Plaid when Plaid reports that it no longer exists there,
// and only then. A user withdrawing access to a link at their bank does not
// make it gone: its next action is RECONNECT, and an update may restore it
// (see StartBankUpdate). A user who deletes the connection through Plaid's
// own portal, though, can make it gone, and the link is then removed and
// disappears from the list, as below.
//
// When Flipcash learns that a link is gone at Plaid, it removes the link and
// all its accounts from Flipcash, everywhere Flipcash uses them, as
// RemoveBankLink does, except that nothing is left to disconnect at Plaid.
// It learns this from a notice Plaid sends it, or when it reads the link
// from Plaid: in ListBankLinks, which then does not list the link, and in
// StartBankUpdate and CompleteBankUpdate, which then answer NOT_FOUND. It
// does so only when Plaid reports that the link is gone: when Plaid cannot
// say, for example because it answers with an error or not in time, the link
// is kept, and listed with next action TRY_LATER. A client therefore sees
// such a bank disappear from its list; the user links it again with
// StartBankLink, which makes a new link with a new id. A link removed this
// way no longer counts toward the limit on linked banks.
//
// Flipcash keeps a link's accounts in line with what the user shares at
// Plaid, adding the ones newly shared and removing the ones no longer shared,
// when it reads the link from Plaid: CompleteBankUpdate does so at once after
// an update, and Flipcash also reads links from Plaid from time to time (see
// ListBankLinks), so a client that did not get to call CompleteBankUpdate
// sees the change in a later list. When Plaid sends a notice that the user
// withdrew access to a single account at their bank, Flipcash removes that
// account then too. A failed read never changes the accounts Flipcash holds:
// only what Plaid reports, in a read that succeeds or in such a notice, adds
// or removes accounts.
//
// Flipcash learns a link's state, its next action and relink_by, both when
// it reads the link from Plaid and from notices Plaid sends it, and keeps
// the latest it has learned (see BankLink.next_action). When a read from
// Plaid fails without Plaid reporting a problem with the link or the bank,
// though, the link's next action is TRY_LATER until a later read or notice
// succeeds, whatever Flipcash had learned before. Such a link is read again
// when the caller next lists their links (see ListBankLinks).
//
// No event or push announces a change in a link's next action, including
// one Flipcash learns of from a notice, or a link removed because it is gone
// at Plaid: a client lists the links again when the user next needs them,
// for example when they start to cash out.
//
// Bank linking is for users in the United States, with accounts at banks in
// the United States. StartBankLink, CompleteBankLink and StartBankUpdate
// answer DENIED when it is not available to the caller. A caller who holds
// links can always list them, bring them in line with CompleteBankUpdate,
// and remove a whole bank with RemoveBankLink, even once bank linking is no
// longer available to them, so Flipcash never keeps a link the user cannot
// see or disconnect: ListBankLinks, CompleteBankUpdate and RemoveBankLink do
// not answer DENIED today. Removing a single account needs an update (see
// StartBankUpdate), which DENIED can block.
type BankServer interface {
	// StartBankLink returns a link token for opening Plaid Link to link a new
	// bank.
	//
	// Link is set up so that:
	//
	//   - it offers banks in the United States only;
	//   - Flipcash links checking and savings accounts only, and the user
	//     picks one or more of them. A bank's own sign-in screen may still
	//     show the user other accounts, but only checking and savings accounts
	//     are linked;
	//   - accounts are verified instantly within Link, usually by the user
	//     signing in to their bank (at some banks also entering their account
	//     and routing numbers for Plaid to match), and never by small test
	//     deposits. A completed link is usable at once: there is no pending
	//     state.
	//
	// Linking a new bank requires the caller to be verified (see Bank). It
	// is checked here so that the user does not go through Link for nothing.
	// A caller who is not verified gets KYC_REQUIRED and no token; the
	// client takes the user through verification and then calls
	// StartBankLink again. A caller Coinbase has blocked gets DENIED.
	// CompleteBankLink checks verification again before it uses the public
	// token.
	//
	// When more than one refusal applies, the caller gets the first of
	// DENIED, KYC_REQUIRED, LIMIT_REACHED and RATE_LIMITED. Verification
	// comes before the limits, so a caller who cannot link a bank anyway is
	// never asked to remove one.
	//
	// To add an account at a bank the user has already linked, a client uses
	// StartBankUpdate instead: linking that bank again replaces its link's
	// accounts with the ones the user picks in Link (see CompleteBankLink).
	// So a client shows the user the banks they have linked before it opens
	// Link for a new bank, and sends them to an update for a bank they
	// already have, unless an update for it cannot be made, when linking
	// it again is how it is repaired (see BankLink.next_action).
	//
	// A user may have at most a server-set number of linked banks, today 5,
	// whatever their next action; a link removed because it is gone at Plaid
	// no longer counts (see Bank). The limit applies to every StartBankLink.
	// A caller who already has that many gets LIMIT_REACHED and no token;
	// the client has the user remove one first (see RemoveBankLink), or, to
	// change the accounts shared at a bank already linked, uses
	// StartBankUpdate, which the limit does not affect. A link gone at Plaid
	// that Flipcash has not noticed yet may still count until Flipcash learns
	// of it (see Bank). The check here only spares the user a Link session
	// that could not complete: CompleteBankLink is what enforces the limit.
	//
	// A user may also link banks only a server-set number of times in a
	// recent period. Every completed link counts, including one carried over
	// to a bank already linked (see CompleteBankLink) and one since removed,
	// so removing a link does not make room for another here. So does an
	// attempt answered NO_ELIGIBLE_ACCOUNTS, though nothing was linked. A
	// caller who has linked banks that many times gets RATE_LIMITED and no
	// token; the client tells the user to try again later, for example
	// tomorrow. Only StartBankLink checks this: a link completed with a link
	// token already issued is not refused for it, and StartBankUpdate, which
	// makes no new connection to the bank, is not affected.
	//
	// A link token is short-lived. A client gets a new one each time it
	// opens Link and does not keep one for later.
	StartBankLink(context.Context, *StartBankLinkRequest) (*StartBankLinkResponse, error)
	// CompleteBankLink links the bank the user picked in Link, with the
	// accounts they picked, and returns the link.
	//
	// The client sends the public token from Link's onSuccess callback. The
	// server uses it and reads the bank and the accounts the user shared from
	// Plaid: everything Flipcash keeps about the link and its accounts comes
	// from Plaid, not from the client. A link holds at most 16 accounts, and
	// accounts the user shared beyond that are not held (see
	// BankLink.accounts).
	//
	// The caller must be verified, as for StartBankLink. This is checked
	// first, before the public token is used: a caller Coinbase has blocked
	// gets DENIED, and otherwise one who is not verified gets KYC_REQUIRED.
	// Either way nothing is linked, and Flipcash keeps and uses nothing from
	// Link. A link the caller already has at that bank, if any, is not
	// changed by Flipcash, but may now need repair, since at some banks
	// linking again may have broken it inside Link (see below): its next
	// action shows that once Flipcash learns of it (see Bank). The check
	// covers a caller whose verification changed after StartBankLink, and a
	// client that skipped StartBankLink. Like any answer, it is remembered
	// against the idempotency key (see IdempotencyKey).
	//
	// Only checking and savings accounts held in US dollars at a bank in the
	// United States can be linked; any other account the user shared is not
	// held. When none of the accounts the user shared can be linked, nothing
	// is linked and the answer is NO_ELIGIBLE_ACCOUNTS: the new connection is
	// disconnected at Plaid, and a link the caller already has at that bank
	// is not carried over and is otherwise not changed by Flipcash. At some
	// banks, though, linking again may have broken that link inside Link
	// (see below), so it may now need repair: Flipcash reads it from Plaid
	// again afterwards, so its next action shows that. This is checked
	// before the limit on linked banks, so the answer is NO_ELIGIBLE_ACCOUNTS
	// even when the caller is also at the limit.
	//
	// When the caller already has a link at that bank, as Plaid identifies
	// banks, and the user shared at least one account that can be linked,
	// that link is carried over to the new connection: it keeps its id and
	// linked_at, its connection to the bank is replaced by the new one, its
	// accounts become the ones the user just shared, and the old connection
	// is disconnected at Plaid. The answer is OK, with that link. This is
	// how a bank already linked is always handled when such an account is
	// shared, whatever the existing link's next action, including a link
	// gone at Plaid that Flipcash has not noticed yet. At some banks, linking
	// the same bank again breaks the existing link inside Link, before
	// Flipcash sees anything, so the new connection is the one that works.
	// The carried-over link's accounts are matched to the ones it held only by
	// Plaid's ids (see BankAccountId), so most of them get new ids and
	// linked_at, except at banks that provide Plaid's persistent account id.
	//
	// Linking a bank again therefore replaces that link's accounts with the
	// ones the user just picked. To add an account at a bank already linked,
	// a client uses StartBankUpdate instead (see StartBankLink).
	//
	// The limit on linked banks and the one link per bank, as Plaid
	// identifies banks, are checked atomically with storing the link,
	// concurrent requests included. A bank already linked is carried over
	// whatever the limit, and adds nothing toward it. A new bank when the
	// caller already has as many linked banks as allowed, which only other
	// links completed after this link's token was issued can lead to, is
	// answered LIMIT_REACHED after the public token was used: the new
	// connection is disconnected at Plaid and nothing is kept.
	//
	// A client retries it with the same idempotency key until it gets a
	// result (see IdempotencyKey).
	CompleteBankLink(context.Context, *CompleteBankLinkRequest) (*CompleteBankLinkResponse, error)
	// ListBankLinks lists the caller's linked banks, oldest linked first,
	// each with its accounts. It is not paged.
	//
	// A link's next action and accounts reflect what Flipcash learned from
	// Plaid recently, from reading the link or from a notice Plaid sent (see
	// Bank). Flipcash reads links from Plaid from time to time and keeps the
	// latest it has learned: the server may return what it last learned
	// rather than ask Plaid again, since Plaid limits how often it can be
	// asked about a link (except for TRY_LATER, below). Plaid itself does
	// not check most of a link's details with the bank often, so most
	// problems with a link reach Flipcash through Plaid's notices. When
	// Flipcash reads a link from Plaid, its next action is refreshed and its
	// accounts are brought in line with the ones the user shares at Plaid,
	// as CompleteBankUpdate does: accounts newly shared are added, accounts
	// no longer shared are removed from Flipcash, everywhere Flipcash uses
	// them, and accounts still shared keep their ids and linked_at when
	// Flipcash can match them (see BankAccountId). So a change the user made
	// in Link to the accounts they share shows here later, even when the
	// client did not call CompleteBankUpdate after it.
	//
	// A link Plaid reports gone is removed (see Bank) and not listed.
	// Listing removes no other link: a link at which the user shares no
	// account any more is listed with no accounts, and its next action says
	// what the user does about it (see BankLink.NextAction.CHOOSE_ACCOUNT).
	//
	// A link whose accounts cannot be read from Plaid is listed with the
	// accounts Flipcash last held for it, since a failed read never changes
	// them (see Bank). When Plaid reports a problem with the link, its
	// next_action is the one that fits; when Plaid reports that the bank is
	// temporarily unavailable, or Flipcash gets no answer from Plaid, for
	// example because Plaid did not answer in time, it is TRY_LATER. The call
	// still answers OK.
	//
	// A link whose next action is TRY_LATER is read from Plaid again when
	// the caller next lists their links, however recently Flipcash last
	// tried, so listing again later refreshes it. When Plaid's limit on how
	// often a link can be read does not allow a read yet, the link stays
	// TRY_LATER.
	ListBankLinks(context.Context, *ListBankLinksRequest) (*ListBankLinksResponse, error)
	// StartBankUpdate returns a link token for opening Plaid Link in update
	// mode for one of the caller's links. Link has the user sign in to their
	// bank or consent again, and shows them their accounts at the bank: they
	// may share more of them with Flipcash or stop sharing some. It is how a
	// client renews a link that is expected to stop working (next_action
	// RENEW), repairs a link whose next action is RECONNECT, FIX_AT_BANK or
	// CHOOSE_ACCOUNT, adds a second account at a bank already linked, and
	// removes a single account: an account the user unticks is no longer
	// shared with Flipcash at Plaid, and Flipcash removes it (see
	// CompleteBankUpdate). If the user unticks every account, the link is
	// kept with no accounts. To remove a whole bank, a client uses
	// RemoveBankLink. A link's next_action says when to ask the user to
	// renew or repair it.
	//
	// It may be called whatever the link's next action. When the link turns
	// out to be gone at Plaid, it is removed (see Bank) and the answer is
	// NOT_FOUND: the user links the bank again with StartBankLink. Plaid may
	// also refuse to open an update for a link: the answer is then
	// UPDATE_UNAVAILABLE, with no token, and the user links the bank again
	// with StartBankLink, which carries the link over (it keeps its id; see
	// CompleteBankLink). A user at the limit on linked banks, whom
	// StartBankLink answers LIMIT_REACHED, removes the bank first (see
	// RemoveBankLink) and then links it again. When Link finishes with
	// onSuccess, the client calls CompleteBankUpdate.
	// Link tokens are as for StartBankLink: short-lived, a new one each time
	// Link opens.
	StartBankUpdate(context.Context, *StartBankUpdateRequest) (*StartBankUpdateResponse, error)
	// CompleteBankUpdate brings Flipcash's accounts for a link in line with
	// the ones the user shares at Plaid, after Link finishes in update mode,
	// and returns the link. Nothing from Link is sent. It always reads the
	// link from Plaid at once, however recently Flipcash last read it (see
	// ListBankLinks), so that the change shows immediately.
	//
	// A client calls it only after Link finishes in update mode with
	// onSuccess, never after Link exits otherwise. A call also clears a
	// disconnect Plaid had announced for the link, since Plaid reports
	// nothing when an update has dealt with one: the link's RENEW that came
	// from Plaid's warning ends, and so does its relink_by when it came from
	// the warning (see BankLink.relink_by). It is cleared even when the
	// link then cannot be read from Plaid.
	//
	// Accounts the user now shares that Flipcash does not hold are added.
	// Accounts the user no longer shares are removed from Flipcash,
	// everywhere Flipcash uses them. Accounts still shared keep their ids
	// and linked_at when Flipcash can match them by Plaid's ids; one it
	// cannot match is treated as removed and added anew (see BankAccountId).
	//
	// When the user shares no account at the bank any more, the link is
	// kept, and returned with no accounts, as ListBankLinks lists it; its
	// next action says what the user does next (see
	// BankLink.NextAction.CHOOSE_ACCOUNT). Flipcash never removes a link for
	// having no accounts: the user may share one again later.
	//
	// When the link turns out to be gone at Plaid, it is removed (see Bank)
	// and the answer is NOT_FOUND. When the link's accounts cannot be read
	// because Plaid reports a problem with the link or that the bank is
	// temporarily unavailable, its accounts are unchanged and the link is
	// returned with result OK, with the next_action that fits (TRY_LATER
	// for the bank being unavailable). When Flipcash cannot get an answer
	// from Plaid at all, for example because Plaid cannot be reached, does
	// not answer in time or is limiting how often the link can be read, the
	// call fails with an error: the accounts Flipcash holds are unchanged,
	// and the link's next action is TRY_LATER until a later read or notice
	// succeeds (see Bank). The client may call it again.
	//
	// Skipping it is safe for the accounts, which a later ListBankLinks
	// brings in line, but may leave a renewed link showing RENEW until
	// relink_by. It is safe to repeat. Plaid limits how often a link can be
	// read, so a client calls it once after each update rather than
	// repeatedly; calls repeated too quickly may fail with an error as
	// above.
	CompleteBankUpdate(context.Context, *CompleteBankUpdateRequest) (*CompleteBankUpdateResponse, error)
	// RemoveBankLink disconnects one of the caller's banks at Plaid and
	// removes the link and all its accounts from Flipcash, everywhere
	// Flipcash uses them. Once removed, the bank no longer counts toward the
	// limit on linked banks, and may be linked again. To remove a single
	// account and keep the rest, the user unticks it in an update instead
	// (see StartBankUpdate).
	//
	// When Flipcash cannot disconnect the bank at Plaid just then, the call
	// fails with an error and nothing is removed: the bank stays linked and
	// listed, and still counts toward the limit on linked banks. The client
	// tells the user it could not be removed, and they may try again. A link
	// already gone at Plaid is not such a failure: it is removed, and the
	// answer is OK.
	//
	// It is idempotent: a caller with no such link, for example one who has
	// already removed it, or whose link Flipcash removed because it was gone
	// at Plaid (see Bank), gets OK too.
	RemoveBankLink(context.Context, *RemoveBankLinkRequest) (*RemoveBankLinkResponse, error)
	mustEmbedUnimplementedBankServer()
}

